<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<bpmn:definitions xmlns:bpmn="http://www.omg.org/spec/BPMN/20100524/MODEL" xmlns:bpmndi="http://www.omg.org/spec/BPMN/20100524/DI" xmlns:dc="http://www.omg.org/spec/DD/20100524/DC" xmlns:di="http://www.omg.org/spec/DD/20100524/DI" xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance" xmlns:color="http://www.omg.org/spec/BPMN/non-normative/color/1.0" id="Definitions_sp" targetNamespace="http://si212.umich.edu/schema/bpmn" exporter="Camunda Modeler" exporterVersion="5.50.1">
  <bpmn:collaboration id="C_SP">
    <bpmn:participant id="P_SP" name="obra/superpowers v6.3.0 — design and implement one chunk of work" processRef="Proc_SP" />
  </bpmn:collaboration>
  <bpmn:process id="Proc_SP" isExecutable="false">
    <bpmn:documentation>Drawn from the skill files in obra/superpowers at v6.3.0 (commit b36e082, 2026-08-12), not from the README. Where the two disagree the skills win; the disagreements are recorded in the documentation of the elements concerned.

One run is one chunk: a single request arriving, through a design and a plan, to a merged branch or an open PR.

VOCABULARY. The granularity chain, largest first:
  assignment  — 3 to 5 chunks (a final project, maybe 10)
  chunk       — one run of this pool: 5 to 7 tasks, ~30 steps, 1 to 2.5 hours
  task        — superpowers' word: ~5 steps, one independently testable deliverable
  step        — superpowers' word: one action, 2 to 5 minutes

"Chunk" is ours. Superpowers has no word for this unit — it names it only by its artifacts, a spec and a plan and a branch, which is of a piece with having no planning layer. Words we did NOT reuse, and why: "project" means the repo throughout superpowers ("explore project context", "the project's full test suite"); "unit" is taken twice over, for code structure and in the sentence "a task is the smallest unit that carries its own test cycle"; "cycle" is the TDD red-green-refactor loop, one level below a task; "spec" is a document, and only the architectural path writes one at all.

ARCHIVE CAVEAT: three early plans (2026-01-22, 2026-02-19, 2026-03-11) use "## Chunk N" headings for groups of tasks WITHIN one plan — a smaller sense, from a convention dropped after March. It appears nowhere in the current skills. If those plans are ever handed out as worked specimens, that difference needs one sentence of warning.</bpmn:documentation>
    <bpmn:laneSet id="LS_SP">
      <bpmn:lane id="L_human" name="HUMAN operator">
        <bpmn:flowNodeRef>Start_req</bpmn:flowNodeRef>
        <bpmn:flowNodeRef>End_integrated</bpmn:flowNodeRef>
        <bpmn:flowNodeRef>Sub_finish</bpmn:flowNodeRef>
      </bpmn:lane>
      <bpmn:lane id="L_agent" name="AI AGENT">
        <bpmn:flowNodeRef>Gate_merge_in</bpmn:flowNodeRef>
        <bpmn:flowNodeRef>Gate_classify</bpmn:flowNodeRef>
        <bpmn:flowNodeRef>Task_spike</bpmn:flowNodeRef>
        <bpmn:flowNodeRef>Gate_upgrade_spike</bpmn:flowNodeRef>
        <bpmn:flowNodeRef>End_answered</bpmn:flowNodeRef>
        <bpmn:flowNodeRef>Task_bounded</bpmn:flowNodeRef>
        <bpmn:flowNodeRef>Gate_upgrade_bounded</bpmn:flowNodeRef>
        <bpmn:flowNodeRef>End_delivered</bpmn:flowNodeRef>
        <bpmn:flowNodeRef>Sub_brainstorm</bpmn:flowNodeRef>
        <bpmn:flowNodeRef>Sub_plan</bpmn:flowNodeRef>
        <bpmn:flowNodeRef>Sub_execute</bpmn:flowNodeRef>
      </bpmn:lane>
      <bpmn:lane id="L_program" name="PROGRAM">
        <bpmn:flowNodeRef>Prog_suite</bpmn:flowNodeRef>
        <bpmn:flowNodeRef>Gate_green</bpmn:flowNodeRef>
        <bpmn:flowNodeRef>End_red</bpmn:flowNodeRef>
      </bpmn:lane>
    </bpmn:laneSet>
    <bpmn:startEvent id="Start_req" name="ready to start&#10;a chunk of work">
      <bpmn:documentation>Superpowers begins here, with a human bringing it one thing to build. It says nothing about how that thing was chosen — there is no product-planning, roadmap or backlog step anywhere in its skills library.</bpmn:documentation>
      <bpmn:outgoing>f1</bpmn:outgoing>
    </bpmn:startEvent>
    <bpmn:exclusiveGateway id="Gate_merge_in">
      <bpmn:documentation>Unnamed, so a reader knows it is a merge and not a decision. It exists because the path ratchet (see Gate_upgrade_spike and Gate_upgrade_bounded) sends work back to be reclassified, and one gateway may never both join and fork.</bpmn:documentation>
      <bpmn:incoming>f1</bpmn:incoming>
      <bpmn:incoming>f8</bpmn:incoming>
      <bpmn:incoming>f11</bpmn:incoming>
      <bpmn:outgoing>f2</bpmn:outgoing>
    </bpmn:exclusiveGateway>
    <bpmn:exclusiveGateway id="Gate_classify" name="how much process&#10;does this need?">
      <bpmn:documentation>obra/superpowers:skills/brainstorming/SKILL.md — "Three Paths". The agent classifies the request and must say the classification out loud before its first question, so the human partner can override it. Ties go to the heavier path: "When in doubt between two paths, take the heavier one."

Drawn at the top level rather than inside the brainstorming subprocess because it decides the shape of the entire run, and because it is the most distinctive single move in the methodology.</bpmn:documentation>
      <bpmn:incoming>f2</bpmn:incoming>
      <bpmn:outgoing>f3</bpmn:outgoing>
      <bpmn:outgoing>f4</bpmn:outgoing>
      <bpmn:outgoing>f5</bpmn:outgoing>
    </bpmn:exclusiveGateway>
    <bpmn:task id="Task_spike" name="Probe the question and report a recommendation&#10;brainstorming — spike path">
      <bpmn:documentation>A feasibility question whose output is an answer, not code that is kept. The agent presents the question and its probe in two or three sentences, gets a nod, then investigates as cheaply as correctness allows. Anything built stays labelled throwaway.

Implemented by obra/superpowers:skills/brainstorming/SKILL.md, spike path. Drawn as a leaf, not a subprocess: there is no level below to model.</bpmn:documentation>
      <bpmn:incoming>f3</bpmn:incoming>
      <bpmn:outgoing>f6</bpmn:outgoing>
    </bpmn:task>
    <bpmn:exclusiveGateway id="Gate_upgrade_spike" name="hidden&#10;complexity?">
      <bpmn:documentation>The ratchet is one-way: "hidden complexity discovered mid-task upgrades the path — stop, say so, and step up. Nothing downgrades mid-task." Drawn twice, once per lightweight path, because a single gateway serving both would have to join and fork.</bpmn:documentation>
      <bpmn:incoming>f6</bpmn:incoming>
      <bpmn:outgoing>f7</bpmn:outgoing>
      <bpmn:outgoing>f8</bpmn:outgoing>
    </bpmn:exclusiveGateway>
    <bpmn:endEvent id="End_answered" name="question answered,&#10;nothing kept">
      <bpmn:documentation>"The spike works, so I'll keep the code" is listed as a red flag: keeping it is a new request that gets its own classification and its own approval.</bpmn:documentation>
      <bpmn:incoming>f7</bpmn:incoming>
    </bpmn:endEvent>
    <bpmn:task id="Task_bounded" name="Design in chat, get approval, implement&#10;brainstorming — bounded path">
      <bpmn:documentation>Implemented by obra/superpowers:skills/brainstorming/SKILL.md, bounded path. A well-scoped change to a flow that already exists in the repo. Clarifying questions, a short design presented in chat, an explicit yes, then implementation through the normal development workflow with TDD. No spec file and no plan document.

The approval gate here is as hard as the architectural one — "What scales with simplicity is the artifact, never the approval" — but it is a gate inside a leaf, so this diagram does not show it. That is a real loss of resolution and the reason this path is drawn as a stub rather than a phase.

The skills do not say whether bounded work then runs finishing-a-development-branch. This end event asserts that the run terminates, not that the branch is handled differently.</bpmn:documentation>
      <bpmn:incoming>f4</bpmn:incoming>
      <bpmn:outgoing>f9</bpmn:outgoing>
    </bpmn:task>
    <bpmn:exclusiveGateway id="Gate_upgrade_bounded" name="hidden&#10;complexity?">
      <bpmn:documentation>See Gate_upgrade_spike. "It grew, but I'm almost done — no need to re-classify" is a listed red flag.</bpmn:documentation>
      <bpmn:incoming>f9</bpmn:incoming>
      <bpmn:outgoing>f10</bpmn:outgoing>
      <bpmn:outgoing>f11</bpmn:outgoing>
    </bpmn:exclusiveGateway>
    <bpmn:endEvent id="End_delivered" name="change delivered">
      <bpmn:incoming>f10</bpmn:incoming>
    </bpmn:endEvent>
    <bpmn:serviceTask id="Prog_suite" name="Run the project&#39;s full test suite&#10;npm test / pytest / cargo test / go test ./...">
      <bpmn:documentation>obra/superpowers:skills/finishing-a-development-branch/SKILL.md, Step 1. The project's own test command, not a superpowers program — which is why the label carries commands rather than a path into the repo.

This is the machine adjudication that matters most in the whole workflow: the integration menu is not shown until the suite is green. "If tests fail, report the failures and stop — the menu comes after a green suite."

It is not the only one. TDD's red/green cycle runs inside every task in Sub_execute, adjudicated by the same kind of command. The PROGRAM lane is thin here but it is not empty, and that is a real difference from the learning workflow in workflow-v2.bpmn, where nothing is machine-adjudicated at all.</bpmn:documentation>
      <bpmn:incoming>f14</bpmn:incoming>
      <bpmn:outgoing>f15</bpmn:outgoing>
    </bpmn:serviceTask>
    <bpmn:exclusiveGateway id="Gate_green" name="suite green?">
      <bpmn:documentation>Adjudicated by the test command's exit status, which is why this diamond sits in PROGRAM rather than AI AGENT.</bpmn:documentation>
      <bpmn:incoming>f15</bpmn:incoming>
      <bpmn:outgoing>f16</bpmn:outgoing>
      <bpmn:outgoing>f17</bpmn:outgoing>
    </bpmn:exclusiveGateway>
    <bpmn:endEvent id="End_red" name="stopped,&#10;suite red">
      <bpmn:documentation>The skill stops and reports the failures. It does not loop back into the task loop, and it does not present the menu. Drawn as a terminal state because that is what the prose describes; a reader should notice that superpowers has no automatic repair path from here.</bpmn:documentation>
      <bpmn:incoming>f16</bpmn:incoming>
    </bpmn:endEvent>
    <bpmn:endEvent id="End_integrated" name="work integrated&#10;or handed back">
      <bpmn:documentation>One end event for three menu outcomes, because from the outside the run is over either way: merged, PR open, or branch kept for the human to handle. Which one fired is visible on the plane below, not here — a subprocess's outgoing flow carries no outcome.</bpmn:documentation>
      <bpmn:incoming>f18</bpmn:incoming>
    </bpmn:endEvent>
    <bpmn:dataObjectReference id="DOR_spec" name="spec&#10;docs/superpowers/specs/" dataObjectRef="DO_spec">
      <bpmn:documentation>Full name: docs/superpowers/specs/YYYY-MM-DD-&lt;topic&gt;-design.md. Written and committed by Sub_brainstorm, on the architectural path only — the spike and bounded paths write no spec at all, so this object does not exist for two runs in three. A user preference for spec location overrides the default directory.</bpmn:documentation>
    </bpmn:dataObjectReference>
    <bpmn:dataObjectReference id="DOR_plan" name="plan&#10;docs/superpowers/plans/" dataObjectRef="DO_plan">
      <bpmn:documentation>Full name: docs/superpowers/plans/YYYY-MM-DD-&lt;feature-name&gt;.md. Written by Sub_plan. A user preference for plan location overrides the default directory.

Every plan opens with a required header naming the spec it argues from and a Global Constraints block copied verbatim from that spec — the constraints bind every task in the plan, and the task reviewer is handed them as its attention lens.</bpmn:documentation>
    </bpmn:dataObjectReference>
    <bpmn:subProcess id="Sub_brainstorm" name="Brainstorm the design">
      <bpmn:documentation>obra/superpowers:skills/brainstorming/SKILL.md — architectural path.

Below this box: explore project context, a scope check that can decompose an over-large request, clarifying questions one at a time, two or three approaches with a recommendation, the design presented in sections with approval after each, the spec written and committed, a self-review, and a human review gate on the written spec.

Two human gates live in here, and they are the only approval gates in the whole run before the finish menu.

Superpowers' own decomposition step lives here too, as a scope guard rather than a planning phase: help the user decompose into sub-projects, decide what order to build them, "then brainstorm the first sub-project through the normal design flow." Nothing records the others — no file, no path, no return.</bpmn:documentation>
      <bpmn:incoming>f5</bpmn:incoming>
      <bpmn:outgoing>f12</bpmn:outgoing>
      <bpmn:dataOutputAssociation id="doa_spec">
        <bpmn:targetRef>DOR_spec</bpmn:targetRef>
      </bpmn:dataOutputAssociation>
      <bpmn:laneSet id="LB_LS">
        <bpmn:lane id="LB_program" name="PROGRAM">
          <bpmn:flowNodeRef>B_commit</bpmn:flowNodeRef>
        </bpmn:lane>
        <bpmn:lane id="LB_agent" name="AI AGENT">
          <bpmn:flowNodeRef>B_explore</bpmn:flowNodeRef>
          <bpmn:flowNodeRef>B_decompose</bpmn:flowNodeRef>
          <bpmn:flowNodeRef>GB_qmerge</bpmn:flowNodeRef>
          <bpmn:flowNodeRef>B_ask</bpmn:flowNodeRef>
          <bpmn:flowNodeRef>B_approaches</bpmn:flowNodeRef>
          <bpmn:flowNodeRef>GB_secmerge</bpmn:flowNodeRef>
          <bpmn:flowNodeRef>B_section</bpmn:flowNodeRef>
          <bpmn:flowNodeRef>GB_specmerge</bpmn:flowNodeRef>
          <bpmn:flowNodeRef>B_write</bpmn:flowNodeRef>
          <bpmn:flowNodeRef>B_selfreview</bpmn:flowNodeRef>
          <bpmn:flowNodeRef>Start_B</bpmn:flowNodeRef>
          <bpmn:flowNodeRef>GB_scope</bpmn:flowNodeRef>
          <bpmn:flowNodeRef>GB_understood</bpmn:flowNodeRef>
          <bpmn:flowNodeRef>GB_more</bpmn:flowNodeRef>
          <bpmn:flowNodeRef>End_B</bpmn:flowNodeRef>
        </bpmn:lane>
        <bpmn:lane id="LB_human" name="HUMAN operator">
          <bpmn:flowNodeRef>B_answer</bpmn:flowNodeRef>
          <bpmn:flowNodeRef>B_review</bpmn:flowNodeRef>
          <bpmn:flowNodeRef>GB_secok</bpmn:flowNodeRef>
          <bpmn:flowNodeRef>GB_changes</bpmn:flowNodeRef>
        </bpmn:lane>
      </bpmn:laneSet>
      <bpmn:startEvent id="Start_B" name="architectural&#10;path chosen">
        <bpmn:documentation>obra/superpowers:skills/brainstorming/SKILL.md, architectural path. Entered from the classification gateway in the parent: the agent has said out loud that this request is architectural (new projects, new subsystems, changes that restructure how components fit or alter interfaces others depend on), and the human has not overridden it.</bpmn:documentation>
        <bpmn:outgoing>bf1</bpmn:outgoing>
      </bpmn:startEvent>
      <bpmn:task id="B_explore" name="Explore project context&#10;brainstorming">
        <bpmn:documentation>obra/superpowers:skills/brainstorming/SKILL.md, checklist step 1: check out the current project state first (files, docs, recent commits).</bpmn:documentation>
        <bpmn:incoming>bf1</bpmn:incoming>
        <bpmn:outgoing>bf2</bpmn:outgoing>
      </bpmn:task>
      <bpmn:exclusiveGateway id="GB_scope" name="too big for&#10;one spec?">
        <bpmn:documentation>obra/superpowers:skills/brainstorming/SKILL.md, "Understanding the idea": before asking detailed questions, assess scope. If the request describes multiple independent subsystems, flag it immediately rather than spend questions refining details of a project that needs to be decomposed first. The agent makes this call.</bpmn:documentation>
        <bpmn:incoming>bf2</bpmn:incoming>
        <bpmn:outgoing>bf3</bpmn:outgoing>
        <bpmn:outgoing>bf5</bpmn:outgoing>
      </bpmn:exclusiveGateway>
      <bpmn:task id="B_decompose" name="Decompose into sub-projects, then take the first&#10;brainstorming">
        <bpmn:documentation>obra/superpowers:skills/brainstorming/SKILL.md: help the user decompose into sub-projects (what are the independent pieces, how do they relate, what order should they be built), then brainstorm the first sub-project through the normal design flow. Each sub-project gets its own spec, plan and implementation cycle.

Drawn across HUMAN and AGENT because both do the work: the agent proposes the split and the human agrees to it. Filed under AI AGENT, which it mostly overlaps.

Nothing records the other sub-projects: no file, no path, no return. This is superpowers' only decomposition step, and it is a scope guard rather than a planning phase.</bpmn:documentation>
        <bpmn:incoming>bf3</bpmn:incoming>
        <bpmn:outgoing>bf4</bpmn:outgoing>
      </bpmn:task>
      <bpmn:exclusiveGateway id="GB_qmerge">
        <bpmn:documentation>Unnamed, so a reader knows it is a merge and not a decision. The question loop returns here.</bpmn:documentation>
        <bpmn:incoming>bf4</bpmn:incoming>
        <bpmn:incoming>bf5</bpmn:incoming>
        <bpmn:incoming>bf10</bpmn:incoming>
        <bpmn:outgoing>bf6</bpmn:outgoing>
      </bpmn:exclusiveGateway>
      <bpmn:task id="B_ask" name="Ask one clarifying question&#10;brainstorming">
        <bpmn:documentation>obra/superpowers:skills/brainstorming/SKILL.md, checklist step 3. One question per message; multiple choice where possible, open-ended is fine too. Focus: purpose, constraints, success criteria.

Checklist step 2, offering the visual companion, happens here at most once, as its own message, the first time a question would be clearer shown than told. It is a side feature and is not drawn.</bpmn:documentation>
        <bpmn:incoming>bf6</bpmn:incoming>
        <bpmn:outgoing>bf7</bpmn:outgoing>
      </bpmn:task>
      <bpmn:userTask id="B_answer" name="Answer the question">
        <bpmn:documentation>obra/superpowers:skills/brainstorming/SKILL.md. The human's half of the question loop.</bpmn:documentation>
        <bpmn:incoming>bf7</bpmn:incoming>
        <bpmn:outgoing>bf8</bpmn:outgoing>
      </bpmn:userTask>
      <bpmn:exclusiveGateway id="GB_understood" name="purpose, constraints,&#10;success criteria clear?">
        <bpmn:documentation>obra/superpowers:skills/brainstorming/SKILL.md. The agent decides when it understands enough to propose approaches. The skill gives no count and no stopping rule beyond understanding purpose, constraints and success criteria.</bpmn:documentation>
        <bpmn:incoming>bf8</bpmn:incoming>
        <bpmn:outgoing>bf9</bpmn:outgoing>
        <bpmn:outgoing>bf10</bpmn:outgoing>
      </bpmn:exclusiveGateway>
      <bpmn:task id="B_approaches" name="Propose 2-3 approaches, leading with a recommendation&#10;brainstorming">
        <bpmn:documentation>obra/superpowers:skills/brainstorming/SKILL.md, checklist step 4 and "Exploring approaches": two or three approaches with trade-offs, presented conversationally, leading with the recommended one and why. YAGNI ruthlessly.

The skill has no explicit approval gate here: its own flowchart goes straight from approaches to design sections. The human's reaction reaches the design through conversation, so no human step is drawn.</bpmn:documentation>
        <bpmn:incoming>bf9</bpmn:incoming>
        <bpmn:outgoing>bf11</bpmn:outgoing>
      </bpmn:task>
      <bpmn:exclusiveGateway id="GB_secmerge">
        <bpmn:documentation>Unnamed merge. Both section loops return here: a revision after a no, and the next section after a yes.</bpmn:documentation>
        <bpmn:incoming>bf11</bpmn:incoming>
        <bpmn:incoming>bf15</bpmn:incoming>
        <bpmn:incoming>bf16</bpmn:incoming>
        <bpmn:outgoing>bf12</bpmn:outgoing>
      </bpmn:exclusiveGateway>
      <bpmn:task id="B_section" name="Present the next design section&#10;brainstorming">
        <bpmn:documentation>obra/superpowers:skills/brainstorming/SKILL.md, checklist step 5 and "Presenting the design". Each section scaled to its complexity, from a few sentences up to 200-300 words. Covers architecture, components, data flow, error handling and testing, designed as small units with clear interfaces. After a no, this box presents the revised section instead.</bpmn:documentation>
        <bpmn:incoming>bf12</bpmn:incoming>
        <bpmn:outgoing>bf13</bpmn:outgoing>
      </bpmn:task>
      <bpmn:exclusiveGateway id="GB_secok" name="looks right&#10;so far?">
        <bpmn:documentation>obra/superpowers:skills/brainstorming/SKILL.md: ask after each section whether it looks right so far. The first of the two human gates on this plane, and it fires once per section.</bpmn:documentation>
        <bpmn:incoming>bf13</bpmn:incoming>
        <bpmn:outgoing>bf14</bpmn:outgoing>
        <bpmn:outgoing>bf15</bpmn:outgoing>
      </bpmn:exclusiveGateway>
      <bpmn:exclusiveGateway id="GB_more" name="sections&#10;left?">
        <bpmn:documentation>The agent decides when the design is fully presented.</bpmn:documentation>
        <bpmn:incoming>bf14</bpmn:incoming>
        <bpmn:outgoing>bf16</bpmn:outgoing>
        <bpmn:outgoing>bf17</bpmn:outgoing>
      </bpmn:exclusiveGateway>
      <bpmn:exclusiveGateway id="GB_specmerge">
        <bpmn:documentation>Unnamed merge. A spec review that requests changes returns here.</bpmn:documentation>
        <bpmn:incoming>bf17</bpmn:incoming>
        <bpmn:incoming>bf24</bpmn:incoming>
        <bpmn:outgoing>bf18</bpmn:outgoing>
      </bpmn:exclusiveGateway>
      <bpmn:task id="B_write" name="Write the spec&#10;brainstorming">
        <bpmn:documentation>obra/superpowers:skills/brainstorming/SKILL.md, checklist step 6 and "Documentation": write the validated design to docs/superpowers/specs/YYYY-MM-DD-&lt;topic&gt;-design.md (a user preference for location overrides this). On a return from the review gate, this box makes the requested changes instead.</bpmn:documentation>
        <bpmn:incoming>bf18</bpmn:incoming>
        <bpmn:outgoing>bf19</bpmn:outgoing>
        <bpmn:dataOutputAssociation id="DOR_spec_b_w">
          <bpmn:targetRef>DOR_spec_b</bpmn:targetRef>
        </bpmn:dataOutputAssociation>
      </bpmn:task>
      <bpmn:scriptTask id="B_commit" name="Commit the spec&#10;git commit">
        <bpmn:documentation>obra/superpowers:skills/brainstorming/SKILL.md, "Documentation": commit the design document to git. Drawn in PROGRAM because git does the work; the agent invokes it.</bpmn:documentation>
        <bpmn:incoming>bf19</bpmn:incoming>
        <bpmn:outgoing>bf20</bpmn:outgoing>
      </bpmn:scriptTask>
      <bpmn:task id="B_selfreview" name="Self-review the spec and fix inline&#10;brainstorming">
        <bpmn:documentation>obra/superpowers:skills/brainstorming/SKILL.md, "Spec Self-Review": a placeholder scan, internal consistency, a scope check (is this focused enough for one plan?), and an ambiguity check. Fix inline; no re-review. A checklist the agent runs itself, not a subagent dispatch.</bpmn:documentation>
        <bpmn:incoming>bf20</bpmn:incoming>
        <bpmn:outgoing>bf21</bpmn:outgoing>
      </bpmn:task>
      <bpmn:userTask id="B_review" name="Review the written spec">
        <bpmn:documentation>obra/superpowers:skills/brainstorming/SKILL.md, "User Review Gate": the agent asks the human to review the committed spec file before any plan is written, then waits.</bpmn:documentation>
        <bpmn:incoming>bf21</bpmn:incoming>
        <bpmn:outgoing>bf22</bpmn:outgoing>
        <bpmn:property id="Property_127gkwp" name="__targetRef_placeholder" />
        <bpmn:dataInputAssociation id="DOR_spec_c_r">
          <bpmn:sourceRef>DOR_spec_c</bpmn:sourceRef>
          <bpmn:targetRef>Property_127gkwp</bpmn:targetRef>
        </bpmn:dataInputAssociation>
      </bpmn:userTask>
      <bpmn:exclusiveGateway id="GB_changes" name="changes&#10;requested?">
        <bpmn:documentation>obra/superpowers:skills/brainstorming/SKILL.md: if they request changes, make them and re-run the spec review loop. Only proceed once the user approves. The second human gate on this plane.</bpmn:documentation>
        <bpmn:incoming>bf22</bpmn:incoming>
        <bpmn:outgoing>bf23</bpmn:outgoing>
        <bpmn:outgoing>bf24</bpmn:outgoing>
      </bpmn:exclusiveGateway>
      <bpmn:endEvent id="End_B" name="spec approved">
        <bpmn:documentation>obra/superpowers:skills/brainstorming/SKILL.md, "Implementation": the only skill invoked next is writing-plans, which is the next box in the parent.</bpmn:documentation>
        <bpmn:incoming>bf23</bpmn:incoming>
      </bpmn:endEvent>
