    <bpmn:subProcess id="Sub_execute" name="Execute the plan, task by task">
      <bpmn:documentation>obra/superpowers:skills/subagent-driven-development/SKILL.md.

Below this box: workspace and ledger setup (which is where obra/superpowers:skills/using-git-worktrees/SKILL.md is actually invoked), a pre-flight conflict scan over the whole plan, then the per-task loop — dispatch an implementer, dispatch a task reviewer, and a fix loop of up to five rounds each ending in a scoped re-review — then a whole-branch final review on the most capable model, one single fix wave, and deletion of the workspace.

Seven subagent dispatches are drawn on the plane below, off four prompt templates. Each is a seat, a model tier and a cost, and each carries its tier on its label, because those are the knobs a course would want to turn.

WHY CALL ACTIVITIES, NOT COLLAPSED SUBPROCESSES. Each dispatch is drawn as a callActivity — a thick-bordered box meaning "this runs as a process defined elsewhere". A subagent session is exactly that: its own context, constructed by the controller, never inherited. bpmn-js does not drill into a call activity, which is the honest signal here — there is no plane below, only a prompt template you can go read. A collapsed subprocess would promise a plane that will never exist, and would also fail bpmnlint's start-event-required and end-event-required for as long as it stayed empty.

There are no human gates in here at all. The skill is explicit: "Do not pause to check in with your human partner between tasks", and "Rulings, not stalls" tells the controller to decide ambiguities itself and record each decision in the ledger. Exactly four things stop it: an irreversible or destructive operation, a security-sensitive action, a side effect outside the worktree, and a plan so broken that every path forward is a guess. See Note_nohuman on the plane below — those are interrupts that can fire anywhere, not steps, so nothing draws them.

README DISAGREEMENT: the README lists using-git-worktrees as step 2 of the basic workflow, "activates after design approval" — before planning. The skills put workspace creation at execution time (writing-plans: "it should have been created via the using-git-worktrees skill at execution time"; this skill's Setup section). The skills win here, so the worktree is inside this box and not a phase of its own.</bpmn:documentation>
