      <bpmn:sequenceFlow id="xf32" sourceRef="Script_pkg_final" targetRef="CA_final" />
      <bpmn:sequenceFlow id="xf33" sourceRef="CA_final" targetRef="G_finalfind" />
      <bpmn:sequenceFlow id="xf34" name="yes" sourceRef="G_finalfind" targetRef="CA_finalfix" />
      <bpmn:sequenceFlow id="xf35" name="no" sourceRef="G_finalfind" targetRef="G_finalmerge" />
      <bpmn:sequenceFlow id="xf36" sourceRef="CA_finalfix" targetRef="Script_pkg_fw" />
      <bpmn:sequenceFlow id="xf37" sourceRef="Script_pkg_fw" targetRef="CA_finalrerev" />
      <bpmn:sequenceFlow id="xf38" sourceRef="CA_finalrerev" targetRef="X_finaladj" />
      <bpmn:sequenceFlow id="xf39" sourceRef="X_finaladj" targetRef="G_finalmerge" />
      <bpmn:sequenceFlow id="xf40" sourceRef="G_finalmerge" targetRef="X_rulings" />
      <bpmn:sequenceFlow id="xf41" sourceRef="X_rulings" targetRef="Script_rm" />
      <bpmn:sequenceFlow id="xf42" sourceRef="Script_rm" targetRef="End_X" />
      <bpmn:textAnnotation id="Note_nohuman">
        <bpmn:text>This lane is empty, and that is the finding. No human gate exists anywhere in this phase: "Do not pause to check in with your human partner between tasks." Ambiguities are settled by ruling and recorded in the ledger — "a wrong ruling costs rework your human partner can see and undo; a session parked on a question costs their whole day". Exactly four things stop the run and ask: an irreversible or destructive operation, a security-sensitive action, a side effect outside this worktree, and a plan so broken every path forward is a guess. None of them is a step — they can fire anywhere — so nothing draws them.</bpmn:text>
      </bpmn:textAnnotation>
      <bpmn:association id="xas1" sourceRef="Note_nohuman" targetRef="Start_X" />
      <bpmn:textAnnotation id="Note_budget">
        <bpmn:text>NO BUDGET MECHANISM. Nothing in superpowers counts tokens, and nothing stops at a threshold — grep the skills for token accounting and there is none. Cost control here is entirely structural: the cheapest model that fits each role, the five-round breaker, one fixer for the whole findings list, no second wave, and review packages handed over as file paths so diffs never enter the controller's context. Every one of those is a fixed shape, not a limit that can be set. For a student on a $50 cap, that is the gap.</bpmn:text>
      </bpmn:textAnnotation>
      <bpmn:association id="xas2" sourceRef="Note_budget" targetRef="CA_final" />
      <bpmn:textAnnotation id="Note_noprogram">
        <bpmn:text>Programs do the mechanical work on this plane: resolving the workspace, extracting each task's brief, packaging every diff, deleting the workspace. They produce files, not verdicts. Every decision here is the AI's, including two a script could make: "was that round 5?" is a counter and "tasks left in the plan?" is a ledger lookup. The one machine verdict in this phase, the TDD red/green cycle, happens inside each implementer, a called process with no plane to show it on. The project's own test suite runs one box later, in the parent.</bpmn:text>
      </bpmn:textAnnotation>
      <bpmn:association id="xas3" sourceRef="Note_noprogram" targetRef="Script_ws" />
      <bpmn:textAnnotation id="Note_redundancy">
        <bpmn:text>ANTI-REDUNDANT BY DESIGN. One task reviewer per task. One fixer for the whole findings list, never one per finding — "a real session's final-review fix wave cost more than all its tasks combined". One scoped re-review after it, and no second wave. A worker that spawns its own reviewer is "a defect to flag, not rigor". So "how many redundant agents" is a knob superpowers set to 1 and nailed shut: turning it up is a disagreement with the methodology, not a configuration of it.</bpmn:text>
      </bpmn:textAnnotation>
      <bpmn:association id="xas4" sourceRef="Note_redundancy" targetRef="CA_finalrerev" />
    </bpmn:subProcess>
    <bpmn:subProcess id="Sub_finish" name="Finish the branch">
      <bpmn:documentation>obra/superpowers:skills/finishing-a-development-branch/SKILL.md, Steps 2 to 6.

Below this box: detect whether this is a normal repo, a named-branch worktree or a detached HEAD; confirm the base branch; present a three-option menu (merge locally, push and open a PR, keep the branch as-is); execute the choice, re-running the suite on the merged result if merging; then clean up the worktree.

Declared in HUMAN because the phase waits on the human's answer to the menu, and the integration decision is explicitly theirs. The agent does the work either side of that answer, which is why this is a plain subprocess with no type marker.

This is also where the controller's "Rulings I made" list surfaces — every decision the agent took on the human's behalf during execution, read at the moment they choose what to do with the branch.</bpmn:documentation>
      <bpmn:incoming>f17</bpmn:incoming>
      <bpmn:outgoing>f18</bpmn:outgoing>
      <bpmn:laneSet id="LF_LS">
        <bpmn:lane id="LF_human" name="HUMAN operator">
          <bpmn:flowNodeRef>F_baseresp</bpmn:flowNodeRef>
          <bpmn:flowNodeRef>GF_choice</bpmn:flowNodeRef>
          <bpmn:flowNodeRef>F_strandresp</bpmn:flowNodeRef>
        </bpmn:lane>
        <bpmn:lane id="LF_agent" name="AI AGENT">
          <bpmn:flowNodeRef>Start_F</bpmn:flowNodeRef>
          <bpmn:flowNodeRef>F_base</bpmn:flowNodeRef>
          <bpmn:flowNodeRef>F_menu</bpmn:flowNodeRef>
          <bpmn:flowNodeRef>End_Fkept</bpmn:flowNodeRef>
          <bpmn:flowNodeRef>F_stranded</bpmn:flowNodeRef>
          <bpmn:flowNodeRef>F_strandact</bpmn:flowNodeRef>
        </bpmn:lane>
        <bpmn:lane id="LF_program" name="PROGRAM">
          <bpmn:flowNodeRef>F_detect</bpmn:flowNodeRef>
          <bpmn:flowNodeRef>F_merge</bpmn:flowNodeRef>
          <bpmn:flowNodeRef>F_push</bpmn:flowNodeRef>
          <bpmn:flowNodeRef>F_test</bpmn:flowNodeRef>
          <bpmn:flowNodeRef>F_pr</bpmn:flowNodeRef>
          <bpmn:flowNodeRef>GF_mgreen</bpmn:flowNodeRef>
          <bpmn:flowNodeRef>End_Fpr</bpmn:flowNodeRef>
          <bpmn:flowNodeRef>GF_cleanmerge</bpmn:flowNodeRef>
          <bpmn:flowNodeRef>End_Fred</bpmn:flowNodeRef>
          <bpmn:flowNodeRef>F_cleanup</bpmn:flowNodeRef>
          <bpmn:flowNodeRef>GF_refused</bpmn:flowNodeRef>
          <bpmn:flowNodeRef>F_delbranch</bpmn:flowNodeRef>
          <bpmn:flowNodeRef>End_Fmerged</bpmn:flowNodeRef>
        </bpmn:lane>
      </bpmn:laneSet>
      <bpmn:startEvent id="Start_F" name="suite green">
        <bpmn:documentation>obra/superpowers:skills/finishing-a-development-branch/SKILL.md, Step 1, verify tests, is drawn in the parent as the full test suite and its green gateway, so this plane starts at Step 2. The agent announces "I'm using the finishing-a-development-branch skill to complete this work."</bpmn:documentation>
        <bpmn:outgoing>ff1</bpmn:outgoing>
      </bpmn:startEvent>
      <bpmn:scriptTask id="F_detect" name="Detect the environment&#10;git rev-parse">
        <bpmn:documentation>obra/superpowers:skills/finishing-a-development-branch/SKILL.md, Step 2: git rev-parse --git-dir, --git-common-dir and --show-toplevel, captured now because Step 5 changes directory before cleanup needs them. The result tells a normal repo from a worktree on a named branch from a detached HEAD, which sets both the menu and the cleanup.</bpmn:documentation>
        <bpmn:incoming>ff1</bpmn:incoming>
        <bpmn:outgoing>ff2</bpmn:outgoing>
      </bpmn:scriptTask>
      <bpmn:task id="F_base" name="Ask the human to confirm the base branch&#10;finishing-a-development-branch">
        <bpmn:documentation>obra/superpowers:skills/finishing-a-development-branch/SKILL.md, Step 3. Usually named in the plan, the conversation or the branch's upstream. If it is not known, the agent asks: "This branch split from &lt;your best guess&gt; - is that correct?" "Merging into the wrong base is expensive to undo."

The skill asks only when the base is not already known. This diagram draws the ask every time rather than add a gateway for that condition.</bpmn:documentation>
        <bpmn:incoming>ff2</bpmn:incoming>
        <bpmn:outgoing>ff20</bpmn:outgoing>
      </bpmn:task>
      <bpmn:userTask id="F_baseresp" name="Respond">
        <bpmn:documentation>obra/superpowers:skills/finishing-a-development-branch/SKILL.md, Step 3. The human confirms or corrects the base branch.</bpmn:documentation>
        <bpmn:incoming>ff20</bpmn:incoming>
        <bpmn:outgoing>ff3</bpmn:outgoing>
      </bpmn:userTask>
      <bpmn:task id="F_menu" name="Present the options menu&#10;finishing-a-development-branch">
        <bpmn:documentation>obra/superpowers:skills/finishing-a-development-branch/SKILL.md, Step 4. Exactly three options: merge locally, push and open a PR, keep the branch as-is. From a detached HEAD, only two: no local merge.

Discarding the work is not on the menu. It happens only when the human asks for it in so many words, and then only after they type the word "discard". Like the four stop conditions on the Execute plane, it is a request that can arrive at any point rather than a step, so nothing draws it.</bpmn:documentation>
        <bpmn:incoming>ff3</bpmn:incoming>
        <bpmn:outgoing>ff4</bpmn:outgoing>
      </bpmn:task>
      <bpmn:exclusiveGateway id="GF_choice" name="which option?">
        <bpmn:documentation>obra/superpowers:skills/finishing-a-development-branch/SKILL.md: "Wait for their answer; the integration decision is theirs." The one human gate on this plane.</bpmn:documentation>
        <bpmn:incoming>ff4</bpmn:incoming>
        <bpmn:outgoing>ff5</bpmn:outgoing>
        <bpmn:outgoing>ff6</bpmn:outgoing>
        <bpmn:outgoing>ff7</bpmn:outgoing>
      </bpmn:exclusiveGateway>
      <bpmn:scriptTask id="F_merge" name="Merge into the base branch&#10;git merge">
        <bpmn:documentation>obra/superpowers:skills/finishing-a-development-branch/SKILL.md, Step 5, Option 1: from the main repo root, git checkout &lt;base-branch&gt;, git pull, git merge &lt;feature-branch&gt;.</bpmn:documentation>
        <bpmn:incoming>ff5</bpmn:incoming>
        <bpmn:outgoing>ff8</bpmn:outgoing>
      </bpmn:scriptTask>
      <bpmn:scriptTask id="F_push" name="Push the branch&#10;git push -u origin">
        <bpmn:documentation>obra/superpowers:skills/finishing-a-development-branch/SKILL.md, Step 5, Option 2. From a detached HEAD, the new branch is named on the remote: git push origin HEAD:refs/heads/&lt;new-branch&gt;.</bpmn:documentation>
        <bpmn:incoming>ff6</bpmn:incoming>
        <bpmn:outgoing>ff18</bpmn:outgoing>
      </bpmn:scriptTask>
      <bpmn:endEvent id="End_Fkept" name="branch kept as-is,&#10;worktree preserved">
        <bpmn:documentation>obra/superpowers:skills/finishing-a-development-branch/SKILL.md, Step 5, Option 3: report "Keeping branch &lt;name&gt;. Worktree preserved at &lt;path&gt;."</bpmn:documentation>
        <bpmn:incoming>ff7</bpmn:incoming>
      </bpmn:endEvent>
      <bpmn:scriptTask id="F_test" name="Run the suite on the merged result&#10;npm test / pytest / cargo test / go test ./...">
        <bpmn:documentation>obra/superpowers:skills/finishing-a-development-branch/SKILL.md, Step 5, Option 1: verify tests on the merged result. "A green run only proves the tree it ran on."</bpmn:documentation>
        <bpmn:incoming>ff8</bpmn:incoming>
        <bpmn:outgoing>ff9</bpmn:outgoing>
      </bpmn:scriptTask>
      <bpmn:scriptTask id="F_pr" name="Open the pull request&#10;gh pr create, or the forge&#39;s own CLI">
        <bpmn:documentation>obra/superpowers:skills/finishing-a-development-branch/SKILL.md, Step 5, Option 2: create the pull or merge request against the base branch with the forge's tooling, following the repo's PR template, and report the URL.</bpmn:documentation>
        <bpmn:incoming>ff18</bpmn:incoming>
        <bpmn:outgoing>ff19</bpmn:outgoing>
      </bpmn:scriptTask>
      <bpmn:exclusiveGateway id="GF_mgreen" name="merged result&#10;green?">
        <bpmn:documentation>obra/superpowers:skills/finishing-a-development-branch/SKILL.md. Decided by the suite's exit code, so it sits in PROGRAM like the suite-green gateway in the parent. "A failing merged result stops everything."</bpmn:documentation>
        <bpmn:incoming>ff9</bpmn:incoming>
        <bpmn:outgoing>ff10</bpmn:outgoing>
        <bpmn:outgoing>ff11</bpmn:outgoing>
      </bpmn:exclusiveGateway>
      <bpmn:endEvent id="End_Fpr" name="PR open,&#10;worktree kept">
        <bpmn:documentation>obra/superpowers:skills/finishing-a-development-branch/SKILL.md: keep the worktree, because the human iterates on PR feedback there. "It stays until the work lands."</bpmn:documentation>
        <bpmn:incoming>ff19</bpmn:incoming>
      </bpmn:endEvent>
      <bpmn:exclusiveGateway id="GF_cleanmerge">
        <bpmn:documentation>Unnamed merge. Cleanup returns here after the stranded files are dealt with.</bpmn:documentation>
        <bpmn:incoming>ff10</bpmn:incoming>
        <bpmn:incoming>ff16</bpmn:incoming>
        <bpmn:outgoing>ff12</bpmn:outgoing>
      </bpmn:exclusiveGateway>
      <bpmn:endEvent id="End_Fred" name="merge red: branch&#10;and worktree kept">
        <bpmn:documentation>obra/superpowers:skills/finishing-a-development-branch/SKILL.md: stop, leave the worktree and branch in place, and investigate. Nothing has been pushed, so the merge is local and recoverable.</bpmn:documentation>
        <bpmn:incoming>ff11</bpmn:incoming>
      </bpmn:endEvent>
      <bpmn:scriptTask id="F_cleanup" name="Remove the worktree&#10;git worktree remove">
        <bpmn:documentation>obra/superpowers:skills/finishing-a-development-branch/SKILL.md, Step 6, run from the main repo root with the paths captured in Step 2: git worktree remove, then git worktree prune. Only for worktrees superpowers created, under .worktrees/ or worktrees/. A normal repo has nothing to remove, and any other worktree belongs to the host and is left in place.</bpmn:documentation>
        <bpmn:incoming>ff12</bpmn:incoming>
        <bpmn:outgoing>ff13</bpmn:outgoing>
      </bpmn:scriptTask>
      <bpmn:exclusiveGateway id="GF_refused" name="removal&#10;refused?">
        <bpmn:documentation>obra/superpowers:skills/finishing-a-development-branch/SKILL.md. git refuses when the worktree holds modified or untracked files: files that exist nowhere else. A program decides this one, so it sits in PROGRAM.</bpmn:documentation>
        <bpmn:incoming>ff13</bpmn:incoming>
        <bpmn:outgoing>ff14</bpmn:outgoing>
        <bpmn:outgoing>ff15</bpmn:outgoing>
      </bpmn:exclusiveGateway>
      <bpmn:task id="F_stranded" name="Show the stranded files and ask: commit, move or delete?&#10;finishing-a-development-branch">
        <bpmn:documentation>obra/superpowers:skills/finishing-a-development-branch/SKILL.md, Step 6: never --force on the agent's own initiative. Show git status --porcelain -uall and ask: commit them to the branch, move them into the main repo root, or delete them (unrecoverable).</bpmn:documentation>
        <bpmn:incoming>ff15</bpmn:incoming>
        <bpmn:outgoing>ff21</bpmn:outgoing>
      </bpmn:task>
      <bpmn:userTask id="F_strandresp" name="Respond">
        <bpmn:documentation>obra/superpowers:skills/finishing-a-development-branch/SKILL.md, Step 6. The human chooses commit, move or delete. The three choices all end with the worktree removable, so no branch is drawn.</bpmn:documentation>
        <bpmn:incoming>ff21</bpmn:incoming>
        <bpmn:outgoing>ff22</bpmn:outgoing>
      </bpmn:userTask>
      <bpmn:task id="F_strandact" name="Commit, move or delete them as told&#10;finishing-a-development-branch">
        <bpmn:documentation>obra/superpowers:skills/finishing-a-development-branch/SKILL.md, Step 6: carry out the choice, then remove the worktree.</bpmn:documentation>
        <bpmn:incoming>ff22</bpmn:incoming>
        <bpmn:outgoing>ff16</bpmn:outgoing>
      </bpmn:task>
      <bpmn:scriptTask id="F_delbranch" name="Delete the branch&#10;git branch -d">
        <bpmn:documentation>obra/superpowers:skills/finishing-a-development-branch/SKILL.md, Step 5, Option 1: once the merged result is green and the worktree is gone, git branch -d &lt;feature-branch&gt;.</bpmn:documentation>
        <bpmn:incoming>ff14</bpmn:incoming>
        <bpmn:outgoing>ff17</bpmn:outgoing>
      </bpmn:scriptTask>
      <bpmn:endEvent id="End_Fmerged" name="merged locally,&#10;worktree removed">
        <bpmn:documentation>obra/superpowers:skills/finishing-a-development-branch/SKILL.md, Quick Reference, option 1: merged, not pushed, worktree and branch cleaned up.</bpmn:documentation>
        <bpmn:incoming>ff17</bpmn:incoming>
      </bpmn:endEvent>
